  "starterCode": "import numpy as np\n\ndef cv_clip_cosine_retrieval(queries: list, corpus: list, top_k: int = 2) -> list:\n    pass",
  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nThe implementation computes **CLIP Cosine Retrieval** by following direct mathematical principles, leveraging vectorized operations to ensure both correctness and optimal runtime efficiency.\n\n### ⚡ Python Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef cv_clip_cosine_retrieval(queries: list, corpus: list, top_k: int = 2) -> list:\n    Q = np.array(queries, dtype=np.float64)\n    C = np.array(corpus, dtype=np.float64)\n    nq = np.maximum(np.linalg.norm(Q, axis=1, keepdims=True), 1e-12)\n    nc = np.maximum(np.linalg.norm(C, axis=1, keepdims=True), 1e-12)\n    sims = np.dot(Q / nq, (C / nc).T)\n    return [np.argsort(-row)[:int(top_k)].tolist() for row in sims]\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the size of the primary input.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ auxiliary memory for computing and storing output representations.",
